// The Frost half of the runtime. Everything a program calls that is not the
// program is here, except what genuinely cannot be written in Frost, which
// stays in `frost_runtime.c` beside this and says why.
//
// Each function here is written as `extern fn` with a body, which is what makes
// it keep the name it was written under: emitted code calls
// `frost_rt_check_index` by that name, and a function the compiler named would
// answer to nothing. See 12.5a of the reference.
//
// Two rules hold everything in this file:
//
//   - Nothing here may need what it provides. A bounds check that indexes
//     something calls itself; arithmetic that can overflow calls the trap that
//     may be running already. So the bodies use comparisons and whole numbers
//     and nothing else.
//   - What it bottoms out in is the C stub, declared below. Those declarations
//     are the whole of this file's contact with C.

// Writing a fault out, and ending the process once it is written. Both are in
// the stub: the first because a handler may run where stdio cannot be reached,
// the second because it prints a backtrace the platform has to be asked for.
frost_rt_error :: safe extern fn(text: ^i8)
frost_rt_error_int :: safe extern fn(value: i64)
frost_rt_die :: safe extern fn()

// An index against the run it reaches into, answering with the index so an
// access checks inline: `data[frost_rt_check_index(i, len)]`.
//
// One comparison would do it in C, where a negative index read as unsigned is
// enormous. Two say it here, because Frost has the comparison it means and a
// reader should not have to know the cast to see that a negative index is
// caught.
frost_rt_check_index :: extern fn(index: i64, length: i64) -> i64 {
    if (index < 0 || index >= length) {
        frost_rt_error("frost: index ")
        frost_rt_error_int(index)
        frost_rt_error(" out of bounds for length ")
        frost_rt_error_int(length)
        frost_rt_error("\n")
        frost_rt_die()
    }
    index
}

// The same question asked where nothing is answered with: the emitted code for
// a write through an index calls this and then does the write.
frost_rt_bounds_check :: extern fn(index: i64, length: i64) {
    if (index < 0 || index >= length) {
        frost_rt_error("frost: index ")
        frost_rt_error_int(index)
        frost_rt_error(" out of bounds for length ")
        frost_rt_error_int(length)
        frost_rt_error("\n")
        frost_rt_die()
    }
}

// A slice's length, answered for where the slice is built rather than at each
// access: `slice_from` is the one place a slice comes from, and a length is
// settled once while an access happens in a loop.
//
// Trusting the pointer and the run behind it is what makes the primitive
// unchecked. A negative count is not an unverifiable claim but a meaningless
// one, so it is refused here.
frost_rt_check_length :: extern fn(length: i64) -> i64 {
    if (length < 0) {
        frost_rt_error("frost: a slice cannot be ")
        frost_rt_error_int(length)
        frost_rt_error(" elements long\n")
        frost_rt_die()
    }
    length
}
